Bit Manipulation in Java

Bit manipulation is used to perform operations directly on binary representations of numbers.

1. Get Bit

Check whether the bit at a given position is 0 or 1.

public class Bits {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 5;      // 0101
        int pos = 3;

        int bitMask = 1 << pos;

        if ((bitMask & n) == 0) {
            System.out.println("Bit is 0");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Bit is 1");
        }
    }
}

2. Set Bit

Set the bit at a given position to 1.

public class Bits {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 5;    // 0101
        int pos = 1;

        int bitMask = 1 << pos;
        int newNumber = bitMask | n;

        System.out.println(newNumber);
    }
}

3. Clear Bit

Set the bit at a given position to 0.

public class Bits {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 5;    // 0101
        int pos = 2;

        int bitMask = 1 << pos;
        int newBitMask = ~bitMask;
        int newNumber = newBitMask & n;

        System.out.println(newNumber);
    }
}

4. Update Bit

Update the bit at a given position (set or clear).

import java.util.Scanner;

public class Bits {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        int oper = sc.nextInt(); // 1 = set, 0 = clear
        int n = 5;               // 0101
        int pos = 1;

        int bitMask = 1 << pos;

        if (oper == 1) {
            // Set bit
            int newNumber = bitMask | n;
            System.out.println(newNumber);
        } else {
            // Clear bit
            int newBitMask = ~bitMask;
            int newNumber = newBitMask & n;
            System.out.println(newNumber);
        }
    }
}

🔑 Quick Formula Summary
Get Bit    → (n & (1 << pos))
Set Bit    → n | (1 << pos)
Clear Bit  → n & ~(1 << pos)
Update Bit → Set(for pos=1) or Clear(for pos=0) based on condition
